'''Joy Snihur Wyatt Laking''' (born 1950) is a Canadian visual artist and author based in [[Nova Scotia]], especially known for her [[Watercolor painting|watercolour]] depictions of Nova Scotian scenes. Born to an artistic family in [[Ontario]], Laking painted with her mother at a young age before earning a [[Bachelor of Arts]] in 1972, subsequently moving to Nova Scotia where she went on to earn a national reputation. Laking opened a gallery in 1988 at her [[Portapique]] home to display her work, which remains open by appointment as of 2025.

Laking's paintings are characterized by [[Realism (arts)|representational realism]], and have been displayed in Nova Scotia, [[New Brunswick]], Ontario, and [[Quebec]]{{snd}}including exhibitions at the [[Art Gallery of Nova Scotia]] and the [[Royal Botanical Gardens (Ontario)|Royal Botanical Gardens]] in [[Burlington, Ontario|Burlington]]. Her writings include ''Invisible Prisons'', a [[Play (theatre)|play]] performed in Nova Scotia and [[New York (state)|New York]]; and the books ''Colours in Winter'' and ''The Painted Province'', published in 2019 and 2020, respectively. Laking's work has earned her awards such as the Lifetime Achievement Award from the Cobequid Arts Council and the [[Queen Elizabeth II Diamond Jubilee Medal]].

==Early life and education==
Laking was born in 1950 in [[Owen Sound|Owen Sound, Ontario]], to an artistic family. Her mother was a professional painter, and her father crafted [[Marionette|marionettes]] and [[Toy train|toy trains]]. As a child, Laking frequently accompanied her mother on trips to the [[Georgian Bay]] to paint the scenery of the area. While enjoying these trips, she initially felt inadequate due to the difference in quality between her artwork and her mother's.{{sfnmp|Robert Pope Foundation|2011|1p=26}} Laking attended secondary school at [[Owen Sound Collegiate and Vocational Institute]] until graduating in 1969, predicting in a Grade 8 writing assignment that she would be making a living as an artist within ten years.{{sfnmp|The Sun Times|1996a|The Sun Times|1996b}} She graduated from the [[University of Guelph]] with a Bachelor of Arts in 1972, marrying the same year, and subsequently moving to Nova Scotia with her husband.{{sfnmp|Robert Pope Foundation|2011|1pp=29–30|Visual Arts Nova Scotia|2014}} Around the time she graduated, her mother died of breast cancer, which would have a profound impact on her artistic career as her mother inspired her to become an artist.{{sfnmp|Robert Pope Foundation|2011|1pp=25–26|PNI Atlantic News|2014a}}

Between 1980 and 1985, Laking and her husband had three children: Kelsey, Danica, and Yolande.{{sfnmp|Robert Pope Foundation|2011|1p=30}}

==Career==
{{multiple image
 | align = right
 | direction = vertical
 | width = 220
 | image1 = Joy Laking Gallery entrance.jpg
 | alt1 = Entrance of the Joy Laking Gallery
 | class1 = bg-transparent
 | caption1 =
 | image2 = Joy Laking Gallery sign.jpg
 | alt2 = Sign for the Joy Laking Gallery by the road
 | caption2 = The Joy Laking Gallery opened in the late 1980s in [[Portapique]]
}}
Following her move to Nova Scotia in 1972, Laking settled in [[Bass River, Nova Scotia|Bass River]] and began her art career.{{sfnmp|Robert Pope Foundation|2011|1p=30}} In the early 1980s, she illustrated two children's books: ''The Brook and the Woodcutter'' and ''The Man Who Couldn't Stop Sneezing'' by [[Gary Saunders]]. While she enjoyed the illustration work, she considered the payment to be inadequate, and had to seek other opportunities. She subsequently released a set of calendars featuring her paintings.{{sfnmp|Robert Pope Foundation|2011|1p=33}} Laking went on to hold her first solo [[Art exhibition|exhibition]] titled ''Flowers of Nova Scotia'' at the [[Mount Saint Vincent University#Art Gallery|Mount Saint Vincent Art Gallery]] in 1982 and she began to earn a national reputation.{{sfnmp|The Sun Times|1986|The Griffin|2015}} In 1984, her work was chosen to be featured on the cover of the [[Maritime Telegraph and Telephone Company|MTT]] provincial phone book,{{sfnmp|PNI Atlantic News|2016a}} and 50 of her watercolour paintings were put on display at the [[Royal Botanical Gardens (Ontario)|Royal Botanical Gardens]] in [[Burlington, Ontario]].{{sfnmp|The Hamilton Spectator|1984}} In 1985, Laking held her first [[New Brunswick]] solo exhibition at the Owens Art Gallery at [[Mount Allison University]].{{sfnmp|The Times-Transcript|1985}} In the late 1980s, Laking and her husband built the Joy Laking Gallery at their [[Portapique]] home to display her work,{{sfnmp|Robert Pope Foundation|2011|1pp=34}} opening the gallery in 1988.{{sfnmp|The Sun Times|1988}} Her paintings have also been sold at private galleries in [[Halifax, Nova Scotia|Halifax]] and [[Hamilton, Ontario]]. Between 1982 and 1992, Laking participated in six group exhibitions and held nine solo exhibitions,{{sfnmp|Robert Pope Foundation|2011|1pp=34–37}} including a 40-piece [[Watercolor painting|watercolour]] exhibition at the [[Art Gallery of Nova Scotia]] in 1989, followed by a regional tour until May 1990.{{sfnmp|The Sun Times|1989|Joy Laking CV|2024}} A collection of her paintings featuring summer and winter scenes was displayed at the Royal Botanical Gardens in 1990.{{sfnmp|The Hamilton Spectator|1990}}

In 2006, a painting of a pink grapefruit by Laking was chosen to be featured on a postage stamp, as part of a campaign to raise funds for the [[Jewish General Hospital]] in [[Montreal]].{{sfnmp|The Gazette|2006}} In 2007, after overcoming a serious health issue and inspired by travelling, Laking underwent what she referred to as an "explosion of creativity", branching out into other mediums such as photography, [[Rug hooking|rug hooking]], and wool [[Dyeing|dyeing]]. Laking held a dual exhibition with Laurie Gunn in [[Truro, Nova Scotia|Truro]] in January 2007 consisting entirely of photography.{{sfnmp|Robert Pope Foundation|2011|1p=40}}

In 2013, Laking was the community artist at the White Rabbit Arts Festival in [[Upper Economy]] and celebrated 25 years of annual open houses in autumn at the Joy Laking Gallery.{{sfnmp|Truro Daily News|2013a}} In 2016, Laking was named [[Artist-in-residence|artist-in-residence]] at the [[Port Bickerton, Nova Scotia|Port Bickerton Lighthouse]].{{sfnmp|PNI Atlantic News|2016b}} In 2017, Laking travelled throughout Europe and was given the opportunity to paint in the [[Fondation Monet in Giverny|gardens of Claude Monet]].{{sfnmp|CTV News|2017}} In 2021, Laking's work was featured in two exhibitions at the Fraser Art Gallery in [[Tatamagouche]].{{sfnmp|PNI Atlantic News|2021}}

In April 2020, Laking was deeply affected by the [[2020 Nova Scotia attacks]], a mass shooting that began close to her home in [[Portapique]]. The attacks claimed the lives of 22 people, including three of her friends. The impact of the shootings would affect Laking's career for some time, with her telling [[Global News]] in 2021: "After the tragedy, I couldn't see beauty anywhere. (I couldn't paint.) I didn't want to go out in the woods and sit". The [[COVID-19 pandemic in Nova Scotia|COVID-19 pandemic]] exacerbated these stressors. She later began spending five hours per day in her studio as a method of coping.{{sfnmp|Global News|2021}} On 2 April 2022, nearly two years following the attacks, Laking published a poem on her [[Facebook]] page titled ''Our Portaupique'',{{efn|CTV News reports the title of the poem as ''Our Portpique''. The text of the Facebook post refers to the poem as ''Our Portaupique''.{{sfnmp|CTV News|2022|Joy Laking: ''Our Portaupique''|2022}}}} further reflecting on the changes to the community in the wake of the attacks.{{sfnmp|CTV News|2022|Joy Laking: ''Our Portaupique''|2022}}

==Style==
Laking's work is characterized by [[Realism (arts)|representational realism]], with a focus on ''[[En plein air|plein air]]'' paintings which capture the landscapes of Nova Scotia, particularly in the [[Bay of Fundy]] region. She is self-taught in [[Watercolor painting|watercolour]] painting, and is primarily known as a watercolourist; she also uses [[Oil painting|oils]] and [[Acrylic paint|acrylics]]. Laking's style is influenced by her travels to countries such as India, Ghana, Italy, and Portugal, informing her distinctive portrayals of Nova Scotian scenes.{{sfnmp|Splash Magazines|2022|Visual Arts Nova Scotia|2014}} She names [[Emily Carr]] as inspiration for her work.{{sfnmp|PNI Atlantic News|2014a}}

==Writings==
===''Invisible Prisons''===
''Invisible Prisons'', a [[Play (theatre)|play]] by Laking, premiered on 8 March 2013 at the Marigold Cultural Centre in [[Truro, Nova Scotia|Truro]]. The play originated from a project in which Laking interviewed 35 victims of [[Domestic violence|domestic abuse]], creating 14 monologues. The monologues were well-received at a Women's Motivational Luncheon and Laking created the play as a method of further sharing the project. On 17 January 2013, Laking made a presentation to the [[Colchester County]] Municipal Council requesting financial support in the production of the play. Council voted unanimously to grant her request.{{sfnmp|Council Committee|2013}} The premiere of ''Invisible Prisons'' was produced by [[Lenore Zann]] with a cast from the Truro Theatre Society, with [[Bryden MacDonald]] revising the script and directing the cast.{{sfnmp|The Shoreline Journal|2013|Truro Daily News|2013b}} The play has also been performed by the Amherst Comedy Troupe Theatre Group in [[Amherst, Nova Scotia|Amherst]] in 2014{{sfnmp|PNI Atlantic News|2014b}} and the Osler Society Medicine in Theatre Group at the [[Zucker School of Medicine]] in 2020.{{sfnmp|Zucker School of Medicine|2020}}

===''Colours in Winter''===
In 2019, Laking published her first children's fiction book, ''Colours in Winter''. The book was written and illustrated by Laking over 35 years prior to publishing. After sending a copy of the book to [[Pottersfield Press]], a Nova Scotia-based publisher, they agreed to publish it following some alterations to the original work. The 36-page book follows a young girl who expresses disappointment in the lack of colour in the wintertime, wishing for other seasons to come. Colourful [[Snowflake|snowflakes]] begin to fall, at first to the delight of the girl, before she begins to feel there is now an overabundance of colour. When the snowflakes return to normal, the girl begins to notice the various colours in nature around her, such as [[Rose hip|rose hips]], birds, and [[Evergreen|evergreens]].{{sfnmp|PNI Atlantic News|2019}}

===''The Painted Province''===
{{external media
| width         = 180px
| caption       = via [[Audacy]]
| audio1        = [https://www.audacy.com/podcast/archibaldstudio-5463c/episodes/the-painted-province-an-interview-with-joy-snihur-wyatt-laking-bb0a3 The Painted Province, an interview with Joy Snihur Wyatt Laking] (27:25 min; 1 May 2022), from [https://www.audacy.com/podcast/archibaldstudio-5463c Archibald Studio], ''retrieved 19 April 2025'' {{nobreak|{{endash}} via [[Audacy]]}}
}}
In 2020, Laking published ''The Painted Province'', a book featuring 200 of her paintings from 40 locations in Nova Scotia. In an interview with Fran Zell of ''Splash Magazines'', Laking explained her intentions with the book, stating: "I didn't want it to be a [[Coffee table book|coffee table book]], so I made it so it would fit in a [[Glove compartment|glove compartment]] and included GPS coordinates for each location. I thought, if families kept it in their glove compartment, they could say to their kids, 'Joy painted here. Let's go see the spot.{{' "}}{{sfnmp|Splash Magazines|2022}} The book was received positively by Lana Shupe of ''The Miramichi Reader'',{{sfnmp|The Miramichi Reader|2020}} and Elissa Barnard of ''Billie''.{{sfnmp|Billie|2020}}

